After checkout:

  Check if installed:  make autoconf automake (>1.9) zlib1g-dev libtool and libncursesw6-dev (previously libncurses-dev) and optionaly libfltk1.3-dev (for ax25-tools/hdlcutil). Parts of the ax25-tools package will also need fltk and fltk development packages (and are silently skipped on compile if these packages are not installed).

  Then prepare compilation:

  autoreconf --install --force

  Linux with kernel-AX25:
    ./configure --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc --localstatedir=/var --mandir=/usr/share/man
  Linux without kernel-AX25: 
    ./configure --enable-userspace-ax25 --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc --localstatedir=/var --mandir=/usr/share/man
  MacOSX:
    ./configure

  The same line does for all three packages.  --enable-userspace-ax25 is
  libax25's - the interception of the socket calls lives there - and
  ax25-apps and ax25-tools are built the same either way.  They take the
  option regardless, so that one line works everywhere and nobody has to
  get used to a warning; each says on its own configure line that the
  option belongs to the library.

  Do not hand it --prefix=/usr on macOS.  /usr/lib belongs to the system, and
  the library does not even link for it: ld refuses the interposing table that
  a shared-cache eligible dylib may not carry ("Shared cache eligible dylib
  cannot use interposing tuples" - see axsock(7) for what the table is for).
  The defaults are what a station there wants: /usr/local, /usr/local/etc/ax25,
  /usr/local/share/man.

Compile:
  make install

Rebuilding later needs no configure line at all, and typing one again is how a
wrong prefix gets in: config.status remembers the arguments and make re-runs
it by itself when configure.ac changed.

  make clean; autoreconf --install --force; make install; make clean

Only once (if you did not have configuration files in /etc; if you have, they
will be overwritten!):
  make installconf

Build and install libax25 before ax25-apps and ax25-tools; they link against
it, and some of what they call lives only in the newer library.

Where there is no kernel AX.25 stack - macOS, BSD, or a Linux without the
module - the library can serve the sockets itself.  That is a subject of its
own and lives in README-hints-for-non-kernel-AX25-hosts.txt beside this file.
Running the daemons under systemd is in README-linux-systemd.txt.
